[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Styling in CSS3: Visual Effects, Shadows, Gradients, and Masking"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern CSS3 provides robust capabilities for enhancing web interfaces without relying on external raster images. By utilizing native visual effects, shadows, multi-stop gradients, and complex masking techniques, developers can build visually stunning, performant, and responsive designs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. CSS3 Shadows: box-shadow and text-shadow"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Shadows create depth, hierarchy, and realism. The box-shadow property applies shadows to element boxes, while text-shadow applies them to text nodes."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":".card {
  background-color: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: 0 10px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15), 0 3px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.10);
}

.heading {
  text-shadow: 2px 2px 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Advanced Gradients: Linear and Radial"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Gradients transition smoothly between two or more specified colors. CSS3 supports linear, radial, and conic gradients, allowing for rich background compositions."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":".hero-banner {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1e3c72 0%, #2a5298 100%);
}

.radial-spotlight {
  background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(255,255,255,1) 0%, rgba(0,0,0,0.8) 100%);
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Visual Effects with CSS Filters"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The filter property enables graphical effects like blurring or color shifting to an element before it is rendered. This is commonly used for frosted glass (backdrop-filter) and image styling."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":".blurred-image {
  filter: grayscale(30%) contrast(120%) blur(2px);
}

.glass-panel {
  backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. CSS Masking"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"CSS Masking lets you hide specific parts of an element using an image, an SVG, or a CSS gradient as a mask layer. This offers advanced clipping capabilities far beyond traditional border-radius."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":".masked-element {
  mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0,0,0,1) 50%, rgba(0,0,0,0) 100%);
  -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0,0,0,1) 50%, rgba(0,0,0,0) 100%);
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use hardware-accelerated properties like transform and opacity alongside visual effects to maintain 60fps animations."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep shadow color opacity low to avoid muddy or harsh visual rendering."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always provide vendor prefixes for masking (-webkit-) to ensure maximum browser compatibility."}]
